      "id": "jj9a8si"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "qz56q8b",
      "content": "<strong>  (B) </strong>were"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "r5m1nop",
      "content": "<strong>  (C) </strong>is"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "b6m75kg",
      "content": "<strong>  (D) </strong>are"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6ufdyib",
      "content": "<strong>  Ans: (B) were   Explanation: </strong><em>With 'neither...nor', the verb agrees with the subject nearest to it — 'friends' is plural.</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jqfp1yw",
      "content": "<strong>Q.23. </strong>Which sentence has correct subject-verb agreement?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mr31aux",
      "content": "<strong>  (A) </strong>The committee have decided."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0cqs2c3",
      "content": "<strong>  (B) </strong>A number of students was absent."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "07q8mhl",
      "content": "<strong>  (C) </strong>The news are surprising."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q0f5c89",
      "content": "<strong>  (D) </strong>Mathematics is my favourite subject."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e5eekt2",
      "content": "<strong>  Ans: (D) Mathematics is my favourite subject.   Explanation: </strong><em>Names of subjects are treated as singular: Mathematics is, Physics is, News is.</em>"
    }
